 "title": "Advanced 3 \u00b7 FM with a brightness envelope",
 "text": "In lesson 7 the FM depth was fixed. Real struck and plucked sounds are brightest at the moment they're hit and mellow as they ring, so here the modulator goes through its own VCA, opened by its own envelope: the FM depth (the 'index') falls as the note rings. That's how FM bells, electric pianos and plucked basses are made. Two numbers shape an FM tone. The ratio (the modulator's OCTAVE and TUNE against the carrier): whole octaves give harmonic, in-tune tones; a fifth (TUNE 7) is hollow and bell-like; odd values are metallic clangs. The index (how far the modulator swings the carrier: LIN FM, times the VCA): more is brighter.\n\nTry: the modulator's OCTAVE 2 and the brightness envelope's DECAY short (0.2 s): an electric piano; TUNE 7: a bell; the brightness envelope with a slow ATTACK: a brass swell.",
